(Project Summary: Multilingual Language Identification) Tj
T*
(=====================================================) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Overview of the Task) Tj
T*
(====================) Tj
T*
(Our project focuses on identifying the language of short text snippets drawn from) Tj
T*
(multilingual Wikipedia. Each snippet belongs to one of ten target languages across both) Tj
T*
(Latin and Cyrillic scripts \(e.g., English, German, French, Urdu, Kazakh\). The goal is to) Tj
T*
(automatically recognize the language of a sentence so downstream systems—search, content) Tj
T*
(moderation, or localization workflows—can route content correctly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Key Challenges) Tj
T*
(==============) Tj
T*
(Several languages share alphabets and common vocabulary, which makes them hard to) Tj
T*
(distinguish at the sentence level. Some inputs are very short, contain names or numbers,) Tj
T*
(or borrow words from other languages, all of which reduce the number of reliable clues.) Tj
T*
(We also worked with multiple scripts \(Latin vs. Cyrillic\), which can introduce) Tj
T*
(transliteration and encoding edge cases.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(External Resources Used) Tj
T*
(=======================) Tj
T*
(We used the multilingual Wikipedia data provided for the course and processed it with) Tj
T*
(Stanza for sentence segmentation. For modeling, we relied on standard, open-source) Tj
T*
(tools: scikit-learn for the lightweight machine learning baseline, and the Hugging Face) Tj
T*
(Transformers library for the multilingual XLM-R model. These resources allowed us to) Tj
T*
(compare interpretable baselines with a modern transformer approach.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Solution Implemented) Tj
T*
(====================) Tj
T*
(We evaluated three approaches and selected the most effective and practical one as our) Tj
T*
(primary solution. First, we built a rule-based heuristic system that checks scripts,) Tj
T*
(diacritics, and hand-curated cue words. Second, we trained a character n-gram logistic) Tj
T*
(regression model, which learns statistical patterns in spelling and is computationally) Tj
T*
(cheap. Third, we fine-tuned the multilingual transformer XLM-R for sequence) Tj
T*
(classification. The character n-gram model delivered the highest accuracy \(about 97%\)) Tj
T*
(while remaining easy to train and deploy, so it is the recommended choice for the) Tj
T*
(project’s final submission.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Limitations) Tj
T*
(===========) Tj
T*
(Despite strong results, the system still struggles when sentences are extremely short,) Tj
T*
(when they contain mostly names or numbers, or when languages share very similar spelling) Tj
T*
(patterns. The n-gram model can also be sensitive to domain shifts \(e.g., moving from) Tj
T*
(Wikipedia to social media\) and may misclassify code-switched text that mixes languages) Tj
T*
(in a single sentence. The transformer model can help in these cases but is significantly) Tj
T*
(Page 1 of 2) Tj
